# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree, on
# every platform. Editors are configured to match via .editorconfig.
#
# `eol=lf` is what makes this hold on Windows. Without it, a clone with
# core.autocrlf=true — the Windows default, and set globally on at least one
# machine here — checks every text file out with CRLF. That is a lossless
# round-trip as far as Git is concerned, but it breaks tools that are not:
# gofmt only accepts LF, so `gofmt -l .` flagged all 173 Go files on a Windows
# checkout while CI passed on Linux. Makefiles and the Dockerfile in this repo
# are LF-sensitive for the same kind of reason.
#
# These attributes take precedence over core.autocrlf, so nobody has to change
# their global Git configuration for this repository to behave consistently.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never translate, on any platform.
#
# This half is not cosmetic. Git decides text-vs-binary by heuristic — roughly
# "no NUL byte early in the file" — and a PDF can pass that test.
# src/markitdown/converter/testdata/minimal.pdf did, so it was checked out 36
# bytes longer than the committed blob, which shifted every offset in its
# cross-reference table and failed the converter tests with "malformed PDF:
# cross-reference table not found" on Windows only. Fixtures are embedded with
# go:embed and compared byte-for-byte, so they must arrive exactly as committed.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls binary
*.pptx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.db binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.lib binary
*.exe binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
